C = [
("h1", "8. Appointment and the Handing On"),
("p", "Claim: in Paul’s letters elders are appointed by an apostle or his delegate, with care and with proof, and the teaching is committed to faithful men who pass it on. The passages say little of the form and much of the care."),
("sub", "8.1 “ordain elders in every city”"),
("p", "The commission to Titus is the plainest statement. “For this cause left I thee in Crete, that thou shouldest set in order the things that are wanting, and ordain elders in every city, as I had appointed thee” (Titus 1:5). Two things are said. Titus is to set in order what is “wanting,” and he is to ordain elders. The appointment is by the delegate, as Paul had appointed him. Thomas Constable describes the way Paul leaves the matter: “Paul prescribed an organizational structure but left it flexible. He did not dictate the details but left these open for the local leaders to determine.” The volume agrees that Paul does not give the details. He gives the qualifications and the commission, and nothing else."),
("p", "Luke records an earlier appointment in which Paul had a share: “And when they had ordained them elders in every church, and had prayed with fasting, they commended them to the Lord, on whom they believed” (Acts 14:23). The volume cites the verse as a report of what was done and does not take the account of Paul’s journeys further here. Paul’s own statement in Titus is the instruction."),
("sub", "8.2 Care in laying on hands"),
("p", "Paul tells Timothy, “Lay hands suddenly on no man, neither be partaker of other men’s sins: keep thyself pure” (1 Timothy 5:22). The sentence warns against haste, and it gives the reason: the one who lays on hands shares in the sins of the one chosen. The same care is in the qualifications, which require that deacons “first be proved” (1 Timothy 3:10) and that a bishop not be “a novice” (1 Timothy 3:6). Paul also speaks of “the gift that is in thee, which was given thee by prophecy, with the laying on of the hands of the presbytery” (1 Timothy 4:14). The verse says that the elders joined in laying on hands, and it says nothing of the form beyond that."),
("sub", "8.3 The handing on of the teaching"),
("p", "The second letter turns from the office to the teaching. “And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also” (2 Timothy 2:2). Four steps are named: Paul, Timothy, faithful men, others also. The volume reports the sentence and notes what is said of the men: they are faithful, and they are “able to teach.” Paul does not say that they are elders, and the volume does not say it either. He does say that the work is to be passed on, and by whom."),
("p", "The next verses describe the one who does it. “And the servant of the Lord must not strive; but be gentle unto all men, apt to teach, patient, In meekness instructing those that oppose themselves” (2 Timothy 2:24, 25). The phrase “apt to teach” is the one used of the bishop in 1 Timothy 3:2. The volume notes the repetition and does not say what follows from it."),
("sub", "8.4 Submission and respect"),
("p", "Paul also tells the churches how to regard those who serve. “And we beseech you, brethren, to know them which labour among you, and are over you in the Lord, and admonish you; And to esteem them very highly in love for their work’s sake” (1 Thessalonians 5:12, 13). To Corinth he writes of the house of Stephanas, “that ye submit yourselves unto such, and to every one that helpeth with us, and laboureth” (1 Corinthians 16:16). The grounds are the work they do. They “labour among you,” and the word is “for their work’s sake.” Paul does not name an office in either place."),
("ctbl", "Appointment and handing on in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("Passage", "What is done", "By whom, for what"),
    ("Titus 1:5", "Elders are ordained in every city", "By Titus, as Paul had appointed him; to set in order what is wanting"),
    ("1 Timothy 5:22", "Hands are laid, but not suddenly", "By Timothy; the reason is not to share other men’s sins"),
    ("1 Timothy 4:14", "A gift given with the laying on of hands of the presbytery", "By the elders, with prophecy"),
    ("2 Timothy 2:2", "The things heard are committed to faithful men", "By Timothy; so that they teach others also"),
    ("1 Thessalonians 5:12, 13", "Those who labour and admonish are known and esteemed", "By the church; for their work’s sake"),
  ], [0.22, 0.38, 0.40]),
("sub", "8.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Elders are appointed by an apostle’s delegate, and the passage gives the qualifications and no form.",
    "Haste is warned against, and the one who lays hands answers for the choice.",
    "The teaching is handed on from Paul to Timothy to faithful men to others also.",
    "The churches are told to know and esteem those who labour among them, for their work’s sake.",
  ]),
]
